## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates understanding of lock-free inter-process communication under simple multicore constraints and cache coherence protocols. It distinguishes candidates who know the mechanics of atomics from those who understand visibility and ordering guarantees.

## Key Concepts
- Lock-free ring buffers with atomic indices
- Acquire/release semantics for safe cross-core visibility
- Wrap-around via power-of-two masking
- Contention handling and backoff strategies

## Code Example
```c
#define SIZE 1024  // power of two
#define MASK (SIZE - 1)

_Atomic size_t head = 0;
_Atomic size_t tail = 0;
type_t buffer[SIZE];

void enqueue(type_t value) {
    size_t h = atomic_fetch_add(&head, 1, memory_order_relaxed);
    buffer[h & MASK] = value;
    atomic_thread_fence(memory_order_release);
}

type_t dequeue(void) {
    size_t t = atomic_load(&tail, memory_order_acquire);
    type_t value = buffer[t & MASK];
    atomic_fetch_add(&tail, 1, memory_order_relaxed);
    return value;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests deep understanding of io_uring's concurrency model, memory ordering semantics, and real-world race conditions in high-performance I/O systems.

## Key Concepts
- io_uring submission/completion ring ordering semantics
- Cross-core memory barriers and NUMA-aware synchronization
- Per-I/O sequencing for completion tracking
- Ring buffer management under burst conditions
- Atomic operations for completion ring updates

## Code Example
```c
// Pseudo-C outline showing proper barriers
struct io_slot *s = &ring->slots[prod_head];

// Ensure data is visible before updating ring indices
atomic_store_explicit(&s->data, completion_data, memory_order_release);
atomic_store_explicit(&ring->prod_tail, new_tail, memory_order_release);

// Consumer side with acquire semantics
uint32_t tail = atomic_load_explicit(&ring->prod_tail, memory_order_acquire);
completion_data = atomic_load_explicit(&s->data, memory_order_acquire);
```

## Performance Trade-offs
Per-I/O sequence numbers add minimal overhead (8 bytes per operation) but provide strong correctness guarantees. The atomic operations introduce ~5-10ns latency per completion, which is negligible compared to NVMe access times (~100μs). However, under extreme burst conditions, contention on the completion ring indices can become a bottleneck, potentially requiring sharding or per-core completion queues.

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates the ability to reason about memory cgroup enforcement, scalable reclaim under mixed workloads, and kernel-level resource management.

## Key Concepts
- Per-container quotas and deficit calculation
- Per-page state tracking (active, inactive, referenced)
- NUMA-aware page replacement decisions
- Interaction with swap and lazy reclaim mechanisms
- Concurrency-safe quota accounting
- Starvation prevention and thrashing avoidance

## Code Example
```c
// Minimal C-like skeleton of reclaim loop
for (each node in nodes) {
    while (deficit[node] > 0) {
        // Select overcommitted cgroup as victim
        struct cgroup *victim = pick_victim_cgroup(node);

        // Evict pages from victim (prefer inactive, anon > file)
        struct page *page = lru_scan(victim, node);
        if (page) {
            if (try_to_reclaim(page))
                deficit[node] -= PAGE_SIZE;
        }
    }
}
```

