## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of basic synchronization primitives and common OS patterns, especially producer-consumer, deadlock avoidance, and correct semaphore/mutex usage in C.

## Key Concepts
- Semaphores
- Mutex
- Circular buffer
- Deadlock detection
- Boundary conditions (full/empty)

## Code Example
```c
#include <pthread.h>
#include <semaphore.h>
#include <stdio.h>

#define N 10

int buffer[N];
int in = 0, out = 0;
sem_t empty, full;
pthread_mutex_t mutex;

void produce(int item) {
  sem_wait(&empty);
  pthread_mutex_lock(&mutex);
  buffer[in] = item;
  in = (in + 1) % N;
  pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);
  sem_post(&full);
}

int consume() {
  int item;
  sem_wait(&full);
  pthread_mutex_lock(&mutex);
  item = buffer[out];
  out = (out + 1) % N;
  pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);
  sem_post(&empty);
  return item;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to multiple producers/consumers and ensure fairness?
- What are trade-offs vs. using condition variables instead of semaphores?

Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of kernel I/O paths, concurrency, and memory barriers.

Key Concepts

- Lock-free ring buffers and CAS
- Per-slot versioning to prevent double-consume
- Memory order fences between ISR, producer, and consumer
- Wakeup semantics and fairness
- Testing under bursty I/O and backpressure

Code Example

```c
// Minimal producer (ISR) enqueue
int enq(struct ring *r, struct evt *e){
  int idx = atomic_load_explicit(&r->head, memory_order_relaxed) & (RING_MASK);
  if (atomic_load_explicit(&r->slots[idx].claimed, memory_order_acquire))
    return -1;
  r->slots[idx].evt = *e;
  atomic_store_explicit(&r->slots[idx].claimed, 1, memory_order_release);
  atomic_fetch_add_explicit(&r->head, 1, memory_order_release);
  return 0;
}
```

